---
/**
 * What we believe — the church's declaration of faith, in full.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS ITS OWN PAGE rather than a section on /about.
 *
 * /about already carries a "What we believe" section, and it stays there. That
 * section is the *denomination describing itself* — Christian, Protestant,
 * Foundational, Evangelical — and it closes on "We welcome people from all
 * denominations." This page is the *church stating its position*, which is
 * specific and boundaried. Both are true and they do opposite rhetorical jobs.
 * Running the second straight out of the first reads as a bait and switch, so
 * /about summarises and links here instead.
 *
 * It also reclaims an indexed URL. `/what-we-believe` and `/statement-of-faith`
 * were both live on Squarespace and both previously 301'd to /about; the first
 * is now this page and the second points at it.
 *
 * PROVENANCE. The articles are the church's own published Statement of Faith,
 * transcribed from the live Squarespace /statement-of-faith page on 2026-08-21.
 * It is the Church of God (Cleveland, TN) declaration of faith plus the
 * marriage article. It is reproduced verbatim, including "Holy Ghost" rather
 * than "Holy Spirit" and "The Lord Supper" without the possessive, because a
 * doctrinal statement is not ours to copy-edit. If the church wants it
 * modernised, that is Brad's call and it should change here and on paper
 * together.
 *
 * ARTICLE 16, THE MARRIAGE ARTICLE, was a deliberate decision and not an
 * inherited one. On Squarespace it sat on a page linked from nowhere, so almost
 * nobody found it. Publishing it on a linked page is a real change in
 * prominence, and Caleb decided on 2026-08-21 to carry it across rather than
 * quietly drop it. Do not remove it as tidying; that is a decision for the
 * church, not for a build.
 *
 * No crossAt. The crossbar is Home's payoff and repeating it spends it — the
 * same call /about and /watch already make. Scripture on the page does not
 * change that.
 */
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

/*
 * Sixteen articles, in the church's own order. Kept as data rather than markup
 * so the numbering cannot drift from the source: the counter is generated, so
 * inserting an article renumbers the rest automatically and nobody has to
 * remember to renumber by hand.
 */
const articles = [
	'The Bible is the inspired and only infallible Word of God.',
	'In one God eternally existing in three persons; namely, the Father, Son, and Holy Ghost.',
	'That Jesus Christ is the only begotten Son of the Father, conceived of the Holy Ghost, and born of the Virgin Mary.',
	'That Jesus was crucified, buried, and raised from the dead.',
	'That He ascended to heaven and is today at the right hand of the Father as the Intercessor.',
	'That all have sinned and come short of the glory of God and that repentance is commanded of God for all and necessary for forgiveness of sins.',
	'That justification, regeneration, and the new birth are wrought by faith in the blood of Jesus Christ.',
	'In sanctification subsequent to the new birth, through faith in the blood of Christ; through the Word, and by the Holy Ghost.',
	"Holiness to be God's standard of living for His people.",
	'In the baptism with the Holy Ghost subsequent to a clean heart.',
	'In water baptism by immersion, and all who repent should be baptized in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.',
	"The Lord Supper as a symbolic remembrance of Christ's suffering and death for our salvation.",
	'Divine healing is provided for all in the atonement.',
	'In the second coming of Jesus. First, to resurrect the righteous dead and to catch away the living saints to Him in the air.',
	'In the bodily resurrection; eternal life for the righteous, and eternal punishment for the wicked.',
	'Marriage is God-ordained. “The creator made them male and female, and said, ‘For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife, and the two will become one flesh’” (Matthew 19:4–5). Marriage is to be an exclusive relationship, a lifelong faithful union between a man and a woman. This relationship between a husband and wife should parallel the relationship between Christ and the Church (Ephesians 5:23–30).',
];
---

<SiteLayout
	title="What we believe"
	description="The declaration of faith of Brookside Church of God, in full: sixteen articles on Scripture, the Trinity, salvation, sanctification, the Holy Ghost, and the return of Christ."
>
	<section class="bay gtop">
		<p class="eyebrow">What we believe &middot; Brookside Church of God</p>
		{/*
			Three lines with one coral phrase, matching every other hero on the
			site. Declarative, and it does not address the reader — the same rule
			that sent the first round of /visit headlines back.
		*/}
		<h1 class="display" data-split>
			The faith<br />this church<br /><em>stands on</em>
		</h1>
		<div class="gtop__foot">
			<p class="lede">
				This is the declaration of faith of the Church of God, which Brookside
				holds without reservation. It is written plainly and it is not long.
				If you want the shorter description of who we are first, that is on
				<a class="link" href="/about/">the about page</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="bay rule creed" aria-labelledby="faith-heading">
		<p class="eyebrow" id="faith-heading">The declaration</p>
		<h2 class="headline creed__head">We believe:</h2>
		{/*
			One ordered list rather than the term/body definition list /about uses.
			These articles have no headings of their own in the church's text, and
			inventing a term for each one would be writing doctrine rather than
			publishing it.
		*/}
		<ol class="faith">
			{articles.map((a) => <li class="faith__item">{a}</li>)}
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="bay rule after" aria-labelledby="after-heading">
		<p class="eyebrow" id="after-heading">Where this leads</p>
		<h2 class="headline after__head">
			A statement of faith is not the same as knowing Christ.
		</h2>
		<p class="prose after__body">
			Everything above is what this church holds to be true. None of it is a
			substitute for the thing it is all pointing at. If you want to know how a
			person actually comes to Christ, that is written out plainly on its own
			page.
		</p>
		<div class="actions after__actions">
			<a class="btn btn--solid" href="/knowing-christ/">Knowing Christ</a>
			<a class="btn btn--line" href="/visit/">Plan a visit</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
	/*
	 * The list reuses the coral leading-zero counter from /about's `.creed__five`
	 * rather than inventing a second numbering style, because these two pages are
	 * read as a pair and a different counter on each would read as an accident.
	 * It is redeclared instead of shared: site.css holds cross-page primitives,
	 * and a counter used on exactly two pages is not one yet. If a third page
	 * needs it, promote it then.
	 */
	.faith {
		margin: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem) 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
		counter-reset: article;
		max-width: 62ch;
	}
	.faith__item {
		counter-increment: article;
		display: flex;
		gap: clamp(0.85rem, 2vw, 1.35rem);
		align-items: baseline;
		padding: clamp(0.7rem, 1.6vw, 1rem) 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--ash);
		color: var(--iron);
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
		text-wrap: pretty;
	}
	.faith__item::before {
		content: counter(article, decimal-leading-zero);
		flex: none;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.68rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.06em;
		color: var(--coral);
	}
	/* The last article carries a rule above it like the rest; the section below
	   opens with its own, so no bottom border here or they double up. */

	.after { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
	.after__head { max-width: 26ch; }
	.after__body { max-width: 54ch; margin-block: 1rem 0; }
	.after__actions { margin-top: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem); }

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		/* Number over text rather than beside it: at this width a baseline-aligned
		   counter plus a 4-word first line leaves the article hanging off a very
		   narrow column. */
		.faith__item { flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; }
	}
</style>
